  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nThrough the Week...\n\nThe forecast remains on track: Sprawling high pressure over our \nregion is the primary driver of (no) weather heading into the new \nweek. A highly amplified upper- level ridge axis extends along the\nMississippi River this afternoon, while the axis of the surface \nhigh pressure system over the Northeast US extends along the \nAppalachians and over the Gulf Coast. This subsident, dry weather \nregime with large diurnal temperature ranges will continue through\nat least Thursday, and perhaps longer depending on which longer \nrange guidance best verifies.\n\nHave extended the \"Elevated\" fire danger graphic accordingly as \nlow humidity and warm temperatures that have stressed and dried \nout vegetation transitioning out of the growing season. The ridge \nwill be strongest today and Monday, and then should gradually be \nsuppressed by the westerlies thereafter, but do not expect any\nsignificant change in sensible weather at the surface, through at\nleast Thursday. \n\nThe Upcoming Weekend... \n\nBy next weekend, the general idea in the guidance is for at least\nsome moist return flow and possible interaction with a \nshortwave/cold front that could bring at least low to medium rain \nchances. The greatest chance for rain at this point looks to be \nnext Sunday. The forecast for Sunday is 7 days (~168 hours) out, \nas such there is a lot of divergence in the global model guidance.\nContinue to monitor our updates as we approach the weekend and \ntiming/intensity details can be have more certainty and more \nconvergent solutions. Regardless of any rain chances, temperatures\nfor the upcoming Halloween day/night may push 10 degrees warmer \nthan normal. /OAJ/",
